Description: Xge: keep the extension record for the life of the process
 _xgeDpyClose() freed the process-wide xge_info record when the last display
 registered with the Generic Event extension was closed (commit ed8d50ba,
 "Destroy generic event extension after last display is removed").
 _xgeFindDisplay() reads xge_info without any lock and passes it to
 XextAddDisplay(), so when one thread closes its last such display while
 another thread is setting up an extension on its own display, the second
 thread writes into the freed record: XextAddDisplay() stores the new display
 at extinfo->head, offset 0 of a freed 24-byte block, which is where glibc
 keeps a freed chunk's tcache pointer. The heap is then damaged and the next
 allocation of that size aborts ("malloc(): unaligned tcache chunk detected",
 "free(): chunks in smallbin corrupted") or crashes.
 .
 Every other extension in libXext and libXrandr keeps its XExtensionInfo for
 the life of the process and only removes the display at close
 (XEXT_GENERATE_CLOSE_DISPLAY; libXrandr's static XRRExtensionInfo). Do the
 same here with static storage, as libXrandr does: the record is neither
 created lazily nor freed, so no thread can see it change under it, and no
 heap record is left behind either.
 .
 Reproduced with a program in which one thread keeps a display open and three
 threads open a display, call XRRQueryExtension/XRRQueryVersion/XRRGetMonitors
 and close it, all at once: 10 of 10 rounds crashed within 0.2 s with
 libxext6 2:1.3.4-1+b4; Valgrind memcheck reports "Invalid write of size 8 at
 XextAddDisplay (extutil.c:167) ... 0 bytes inside a block of size 24 free'd
 ... by _xgeDpyClose (Xge.c:231) ... by XCloseDisplay".
Author: Daniel Campos Ramos
Origin: vendor, Sparky Stereo OS queue row 762c
Bug-Upstream: none found. Searched 2026-11-21: X.Org libXext main at 4770cdff (src/Xge.c unchanged
 since ed8d50ba apart from casts, NULL checks and unused parameters), its merge requests and issues
 (issue 3, XGE close hooks left behind when libXext is unloaded, is a different fault), Salsa's
 libxext packaging (debian-unstable at 2:1.3.4-1, no patches), Debian testing and unstable
 (2:1.3.4-1; experimental has no libxext) and Debian bugs 1002981, 1024924 and 1102748 (none about
 it). DisplayXR issue 2711 reports the symptom it causes.
